---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **집에서 오래 걸리면 밖으로도 가나.** (`/home-abroad`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ 「문턱」이라 쓰지 않는다. 처음엔 그렇게 읽었다 — 밖 나라 **중앙값**이 1 → 19 로 뛰었다.
 *    그건 중앙값이 50% 선을 지난 것이고, 분포는 11% → 25% → 57% → 63% 로 **기울기**다.
 *    수집기 안에 그 시험이 박혀 있어서, 가운데 띠가 사이값이 아니게 되면 자료가 안 나온다.
 * ⛔ 집에 안 걸린 편과 걸린 편을 **그냥 견주지 않는다.** 안 걸린 쪽은 정의상 전부 밖에 걸렸다.
 * ⛔ 「인기」라 쓰지 않는다. 우리가 본 것은 **차트에 며칠 있었나**뿐이다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-home-abroad.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-home-abroad.json';
import markets from '../../data/wikitip-markets.json';

const TITLE = 'Does charting at home tell you a Korean title will travel?';
const 첫 = data.bands[0];
const 셋 = data.bands[2];
const DESC =
  `Across ${data.titles} Korean titles on Netflix's country top 10 lists, ${첫.reachedTenPc}% of those `
  + `with one or two weeks on Korea's own chart reached ten countries, against ${셋.reachedTenPc}% of those `
  + `with six to ten. But ${data.travelledWithoutHomeCount} titles reached twenty countries without ever `
  + 'charting in Korea at all.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/home-abroad">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Buyers and sellers both use the home chart as a signal. We had the data to check it, so we did:
    {' '}{data.titles} Korean titles across {data.weekCount} weeks of Netflix country lists.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>Home run length against foreign reach</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">
      <b>How a place is counted.</b> {data.unit}
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Weeks on Korea’s chart</th><th>Titles</th><th>Reached 10+ countries</th>
            <th>Reached 20+</th><th>Never left Korea</th><th>Median countries</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr class={b.band === '6–10' ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{b.band}</td>
              <td>{b.titles}</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.reachedTenPc}%</td>
              <td>{b.reachedTwentyPc}%</td>
              <td>{b.neverLeftPc}%</td>
              <td>{b.medianCountries}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>It is a slope, not a door.</b> The median number of countries jumps from
      {' '}{data.bands[1].medianCountries} to {셋.medianCountries} between the middle two rows, which
      looks like a threshold — but the median only records where the middle title sits. The share
      reaching ten countries moves {첫.reachedTenPc}% → {data.bands[1].reachedTenPc}% →
      {' '}{셋.reachedTenPc}% → {data.bands[3].reachedTenPc}%. Nothing snaps; it leans.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The objection: is this just films against series?</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      Series stay on charts longer than films almost everywhere, so a band built on run length could
      simply be sorting formats. We split every band and left both columns in.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Weeks on Korea’s chart</th><th>Films</th><th>Reached 10+</th><th>Series</th><th>Reached 10+</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{b.band}</td>
              <td>{b.film.titles}</td><td class="tot">{b.film.reachedTenPc}%</td>
              <td>{b.series.titles}</td><td class="tot">{b.series.reachedTenPc}%</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Both are true, and the format gap is the larger one.</b> Inside every band, series reach ten
      countries more often than films — and in the {data.bands[1].band} band the difference is
      {' '}{data.bands[1].series.reachedTenPc}% against {data.bands[1].film.reachedTenPc}%, wider than
      the whole home-chart effect. A film that ran six weeks at home still travels less often than a
      series that ran three. If you are reading one number, read the format first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>And the home chart is not a gate</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">
      {data.travelledWithoutHomeCount} titles reached twenty or more countries <b>without ever
      appearing on Korea’s own top 10</b>. They are not obscure abroad; they are absent at home.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x tall">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Title</th><th>Countries</th><th>Chart places abroad</th><th>Format</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.travelledWithoutHome.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm"><i>{t.title}</i></td>
              <td class="tot">{t.countries}</td>
              <td>{t.places}</td>
              <td class="nm">{t.film ? 'Film' : 'Series'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why we did not simply compare the two groups</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      {data.chartedAtHome} of these titles charted in Korea and {data.neverChartedAtHome} did not.
      It is tempting to put those two groups side by side. We did not, and the reason matters:
      {' '}{data.whyNotCompared}
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/home-abroad" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li>
        <b>A top 10 is a rank list, not a viewing count.</b> A title just outside a country’s top 10
        is invisible here at any level of viewing. Nothing on this page measures hours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>We measure presence, not cause.</b> A title can stay on a chart because people keep
        starting it or because nothing displaced it. We cannot separate those, and do not try.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Russia is excluded</b> from every figure on this site.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Weeks are not the same as time.</b> A title with six home places may have taken them
        across two separate runs. We counted places, not consecutive weeks.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/catalogue-depth">All {markets.countryCount} markets side by side</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/screen-split">Series against films, worldwide</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
